<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.11.3: Horizontal Shifts</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01050</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.11.3: Horizontal Shifts</md:title>
    <md:uuid>068b23db-4ef3-4cc0-ba85-c5bdcedb44ad</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <section id="sect-00002"><title>Horizontal Shifts</title>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the following linear functions.</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00002">
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></item>
      </list>
      <media alt=""><iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
  </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Compare your work:<media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_06_01.png"/></media></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00003">What is the direction and horizontal distance between the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is 2 spaces to the right of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00006">How can the function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> be shifted to overlap <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Shift right 4 spaces.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00009">What is the direction and horizontal distance between the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> has been shifted 2 spaces to the left of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00012">How can the function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, be shifted to overlap <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Shift left 4 spaces.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00015">In linear functions, when a value is added to or subtracted from the input value, or the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values, the graph acts as if a horizontal shift is applied. This is another type of transformation to the graph.</para>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Horizontal Shift of a Function</title>
        <para id="para-00016">A horizontal shift “transforms” the parent function into another function by moving the graph to the left or right <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> units.</para>
        <para id="para-00017">Horizontal Shift → <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00018">If the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is positive, the graph of the function shifts right. If the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is negative, the graph of the function shifts left. 

    But, be careful! The sign of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is preceded by subtraction in the equation. When the equation is simplified, the sign of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is changed.</para>
    </section></section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00002">
      <para id="para-00019">Which graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the linear parent function? The parent function is represented by the dashed, blue line.</para></note>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Additional Resources</title>
        
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Horizontal Shifts and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts</title>
      <para id="para-00020">In addition to being shifted vertically, graphs can also be shifted to the right or left. For linear functions, this is sometimes hard to identify because a line that is shifted horizontally may appear to have a vertical shift.</para>
      <para id="para-00021">Let’s shift the parent function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, horizontally to the left 7 units.</para>
      <para id="para-00022">If the line shifts horizontally 7 units to the left, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is shifted 7 units to the left, as well.</para>
      <para id="para-00023">
          <media alt="Horizontal Shift Illustration">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_08_01.png" width="400"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00024">The graph of the parent function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, is represented by the dashed orange line. The graph of the solid blue line represents the line that we horizontally shift 7 units to the left.</para>
      <para id="para-00025">To determine the equation of the shifted line, we use the slope of 1 and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> as a point on the line.</para>
      <para id="para-00026">Substituting these values into point-slope form, we get:</para>
      <para id="para-00027">
        <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <mi>y</mi>
          <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
          <msub>
            <mi>y</mi>
            <mn>1</mn>
          </msub>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mi>m</mi>
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
          <msub>
            <mi>x</mi>
            <mn>1</mn>
          </msub>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
          <mspace linebreak="newline"/>
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <mi>y</mi>
          <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
          <mn>0</mn>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mn>1</mn>
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
          <mn>7</mn>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
          <mspace linebreak="newline"/>
          <mi>y</mi>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mn>1</mn>
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mo>+</mo>
          <mn>7</mn>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
        </math>
      </para>
      <para id="para-00028">So, the equation of a line that has been shifted 7 units to the left from the parent function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00029">But, doesn’t it look like the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> was shifted up 7 units, too? This is because <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is equivalent to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. And this is what makes horizontal shifts difficult to see.</para>
     
      <para id="para-00030"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00031">How has the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> been transformed from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
      <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Solution</emphasis></para> 
      <para id="para-00065">To answer this question, first consider how the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-values, including the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept, are being changed. The function says to subtract 4 from each <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-value. This means the function is being shifted 4 units.</para>
      <para id="para-00033">Next, we need to determine which direction the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-values are being shifted. Let’s look at the graphs.</para>
      <para id="para-00034">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_08_02.png" width="400"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00035">The solid blue line represents the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The dashed orange line represents the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00036">On the graph, we can look at specific points on the parent function like the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept (0, 0) and (1, 3). The transformed line is to the right of our parent function. To determine how far to the right the line has been shifted, we count the number of horizontal units between the lines.</para>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <item>We see that (0, 0) corresponds to the point (4, 0) on the transformed line.</item>
        <item>The point (1, 3) corresponds to the point (5, 3) on the transformed line.</item>
        <item>Each point on the parent function has been shifted 4 units to the right.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00037">Therefore, we conclude the line representing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> appears to have been shifted 4 units to the right from the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      
      <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00039">Use the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept to describe how the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> has been transformed from the parent function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Solution</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00066">We know the shift is 9 units, but we need to consider which direction the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept has been moved.</para>
      <para id="para-00041">
          <media alt="Shifted Function Example">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_08_03.png" width="400"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00042">The orange dashed line represents the parent function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and the solid blue line represents the shifted function.</para>
      <para id="para-00043">Carefully looking at the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts, we see the line representing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> has been shifted 9 units to the left from the parent function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00044">Often the horizontal shift is denoted using parentheses to explicitly identify how the values of the independent variable, or domain, are being changed.</para>
      
      <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00046">Describe how the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>½</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is horizontally transformed to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>½</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Solution</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00067">Let’s rewrite the equations in point-slope form, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msub><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msub><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>:
</para>
      <para id="para-00048">
        <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
          <mtable columnalign="right left right left right left right left right left right left" rowspacing="3pt" columnspacing="0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em" displaystyle="true">
            <mtr>
              <mtd>
                <mi>f</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
              <mtd>
                <mi/>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mfrac>
                  <mn>1</mn>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </mfrac>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>4</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
            </mtr>
            <mtr>
              <mtd>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>y</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>0</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
              <mtd>
                <mi/>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mfrac>
                  <mn>1</mn>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </mfrac>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>4</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
            </mtr>
            <mtr>
              <mtd>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>y</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>0</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
              <mtd>
                <mi/>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mfrac>
                  <mn>1</mn>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </mfrac>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>4</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
            </mtr>
          </mtable>
        </math>
</para>
      <para id="para-00049">So, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00050">
        <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
          <mtable columnalign="right left right left right left right left right left right left" rowspacing="3pt" columnspacing="0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em" displaystyle="true">
            <mtr>
              <mtd>
                <mi>g</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
              <mtd>
                <mi/>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mfrac>
                  <mn>1</mn>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </mfrac>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
            </mtr>
            <mtr>
              <mtd>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>y</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>0</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
              <mtd>
                <mi/>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mfrac>
                  <mn>1</mn>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </mfrac>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mtd>
            </mtr>
          </mtable>
        </math>
</para>
      <para id="para-00051">So, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00052">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> starts at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and moves to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, so this represents a shift of 6 units to the right.</para>
      <para id="para-00053">We can also check this using graphs.</para>
      <para id="para-00054">
          <media alt="Graph Comparison for Example 3">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_08_04.png" width="400"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00055">Examining the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>½</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, on the orange dashed line, we see that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> gets shifted 6 units to the right and lands at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> on <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>½</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, represented by the blue solid line.</para>
      <para id="para-00056">So, we can identify horizontal shifts using either graphs or algebraic equations.</para>
      <note id="note-00003">
        <title>Horizontal Shift of a Function</title>
        <para id="para-00057">A horizontal shift “transforms” the parent function into another function by moving the graph to the left or right <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> units.</para>
        <para id="para-00058">Horizontal Shift → <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00059">If the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is positive, the graph of the function shifts right. If the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is negative, the graph of the function shifts left. 

    But, be careful! The sign of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is preceded by subtraction in the equation. When the equation is simplified, the sign of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value is changed.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00004">
  <title>Try It: Horizontal Shifts and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00060">Tell the shift made to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to arrive at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> using the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts to describe the change.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00061">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00062">Compare your work:</para>
            <para id="para-00063">Here is how to find the shift made to f(x)=x using the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept to describe the move:</para>
            <para id="para-00064">Since the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> = x-5 is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is shifted right 5 units to arrive at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
      </section>
      
  </content>
</document>